    "language_probability": 1,
    "text": "By the summer of nineteen seventy one, there are far more dollars abroad than gold left to cover them. So on the evening of August fifteenth, President Richard Nixon goes on live television and announces that foreign governments can no longer swap their dollars for gold. He calls it temporary. It never comes back. Overnight, the world's main currency is backed by nothing but trust in the United States. They call it the Nixon shock, and it raises a dangerous question. If the dollar isn't tied to gold anymore, why should the world keep holding it?",
